)","culture_narrative":"Employs approximately 17K people. Revenue per employee of $661K ranks in the above the sector median among Consumer Cyclical peers, improving at 8%/yr \u2014 growing workforce leverage. Each employee generates $10K of free cash flow annually. Stock-based compensation of $6K per employee reflects an ownership culture. CEO compensation is 10\u00d7 revenue per employee (modest relative to workforce productivity). Governance score flags moderate compensation governance risk (4/10).","financial_summary":["Burlington Stores, Inc. is a large-cap consumer cyclical business with a $15.5B market cap on $11.6B of annual revenue.","The top line has compounded at +10% over the past three years and +8% annually across the full decade, a respectable pace for a business of this size.","Returns on capital run at 29% against a 9% cost of capital \u2014 a 20-point spread that is the clearest signature of an economic moat.","The business throws off $172M in free cash flow \u2014 roughly 1 cents on every revenue dollar, which is the owner-earnings figure that actually belongs to shareholders, and cash conversion sits at 2.02\u00d7 so those profits are backed by real cash.","On the balance sheet, leverage is comfortably conservative at 0.6\u00d7 net debt/EBITDA, and interest coverage of 12\u00d7 is fortress-like.","Shareholder yield is only 1.8%, a thin return of capital, and insider ownership is negligible, so management has little personal stake in the long-term outcome."],"sector_headwinds":[{"text":"Price sits 52% above estimated fair value \u2014 no margin of safety left, and the cushion against estimate error has been bid away (confirmed by 2 other valuation measures)","sev":"red","cat":"valuation","magnitude":0.5151147764136559},{"text":"Volatile returns on capital suggest the moat is narrower than a snapshot ROIC implies","sev":"amber","cat":"moat"},{"text":"Altman Z in the grey zone \u2014 financial health warrants monitoring","sev":"amber","cat":"quality"},{"text":"Above-average short interest at 9.8% of float","sev":"amber","cat":"sentiment"},{"text":"Insiders are net sellers (100% sell ratio, net $14.4M) \u2014 the people closest to the business are reducing their personal stake","sev":"amber","cat":"insider"},{"text":"Cyclical sectors like Consumer Cyclical tend to underperform in slowdowns","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Consumer staples outperforming discretionary, signaling cautious spending","sev":"amber","cat":"sector"},{"text":"Above-average market volatility (VIX 26)","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Late-cycle dynamics increase the probability of a slowdown","sev":"amber","cat":"macro"},{"text":"Brand relevance decay \u2014 shifting consumer preferences and social-media-driven trends can strand inventory and invalidate positioning faster than product cycles can adapt.","sev":"amber","cat":"thesis_risk"}],"sector_tailwinds":["Reinvestment rate supports 29% organic growth capacity \u2014 capital is compounding inside the business","Trading near the bottom of its 52-week range \u2014 the market is offering a discount","Minimal goodwill on the balance sheet \u2014 low impairment risk","Consumer Cyclical sector outperforming the broader market over the past quarter"],"news_headlines":[{"title":"What Does Burlington Stores\u2019 (NYSE:BURL) Major Relocation Mean?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/midcap/what-does-burlington-stores-nyseburl-major-relocation-mean","date":"2026-11-05","timestamp":1793905080.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.402,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Why Is Burlington Stores (NYSE:BURL) Moving Its Philadelphia?","source":"kalkinemedia.com","link":"https://kalkinemedia.com/us/stocks/retail/why-is-burlington-stores-nyseburl-moving-its-philadelphia","date":"2026-11-05","timestamp":1793838960.0,"origin":"tiingo","sentiment":0.382,"tier":0,"scope":"company","tags":[]},{"title":"Jennifer Vecchio Executes Sale of 1,678 Burlingto